{"id": 13, "ts": 1780807637.146604, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate Agent\u202f1\u2019s clear articulation of Candidate\u202fC\u2019s reliability and crew\u2011atmosphere strengths, but I remain uneasy about the gaps in his communication and willingness to develop\u2014both essential for long\u2011distance operations where continuous learning and clear coordination are vital. Before we elevate C, we should examine whether any other candidate offers a more balanced profile across the full set of criteria.\nFrom my notes, Candidate\u202fA demonstrates strong situational awareness (\u201canticipates dangerous situations,\u201d \u201csees complex connections,\u201d \u201cexcellent spatial vision\u201d) and leadership qualities, which speak to technical competence and decision quality. However, A is also described as a \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d raising concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability.\nCandidate\u202fB is very conscientious, handles stress well, and assesses weather accurately\u2014key for reliability and stress resilience. Yet his \u201cnasty remarks\u201d and \u201cgrumpy, uncooperative\u201d demeanor suggest serious crew\u2011cooperation risks.\nCandidate\u202fD concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, and takes responsibility seriously, indicating strong technical competence and responsibility. The \u201chot temper\u201d and \u201cloner\u201d notes, however, could undermine crew cooperation and professionalism.\nGiven these mixed profiles, I cannot yet endorse Candidate\u202fC as the final choice. I propose we gather more concrete evidence on each candidate\u2019s stress\u2011resilience (e.g., crisis handling beyond calmness), attention to detail (memory for numbers, information accuracy), and crew\u2011cooperation behaviors. This will let us compare whether any alternative\u2014particularly A\u2019s technical foresight or D\u2019s problem\